WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.330
<v David>Before Blair starts, here is what you can actually do for these brokers tonight, as of this build.

2
00:00:08.220 --> 00:00:18.900
<v David>You can pull what every carrier pays in any market in the country, live, on your phone, with the sample size and the date on every number.

3
00:00:19.680 --> 00:00:27.860
<v David>You can mint a rate exhibit with a broker's own firm name on the cover and hand it to them before the panel ends.

4
00:00:28.690 --> 00:00:37.380
<v David>You can show a general agency their whole downline's renewals on one register, with a clock counting down on every group.

5
00:00:38.340 --> 00:00:49.730
<v David>You can open the drug ledger and show a self-funded employer what each drug costs to acquire, what the plan was billed, and who kept the rebate.

6
00:00:49.940 --> 00:00:55.620
<v David>And you can show the paid claim next to the filed contract, side by side on one screen.

7
00:00:56.130 --> 00:01:01.670
<v David>And when someone asks what it costs, the first look is free, no card.

8
00:01:01.960 --> 00:01:05.130
<v David>A seat is one hundred forty nine a month.

9
00:01:05.520 --> 00:01:08.940
<v David>The whole firm is forty nine hundred a year, flat.

10
00:01:09.390 --> 00:01:13.140
<v David>If they want it live on their own book, that is a twenty minute call.

11
00:01:13.170 --> 00:01:14.610
<v David>That is the toolkit.

12
00:01:15.060 --> 00:01:16.930
<v David>Now the questions.

13
00:01:17.390 --> 00:01:19.790
<v Blair>You keep saying the villain is a file.

14
00:01:20.240 --> 00:01:21.370
<v Blair>Not a carrier.

15
00:01:22.320 --> 00:01:23.400
<v Blair>Not a person.

16
00:01:24.710 --> 00:01:26.400
<v Blair>A file.

17
00:01:25.750 --> 00:01:26.160
<v David>Right.

18
00:01:26.990 --> 00:01:30.470
<v David>Most health plans have to file the rates they've agreed to.

19
00:01:30.490 --> 00:01:31.960
<v David>Those files are public.

20
00:01:32.410 --> 00:01:33.540
<v David>They're also huge.

21
00:01:33.580 --> 00:01:36.530
<v David>Almost nobody on the buying side ever opens one.

22
00:01:36.980 --> 00:01:38.410
<v David>There's no villain in that.

23
00:01:38.440 --> 00:01:40.560
<v David>It's just a file nobody opens.

24
00:01:41.380 --> 00:01:44.690
<v Blair>Then start with the part that gets you challenged.

25
00:01:45.560 --> 00:01:55.450
<v Blair>Somebody points at your screen, reads 525 percent, and says 525 percent of what?

26
00:01:54.600 --> 00:01:55.180
<v David>Good.

27
00:01:55.870 --> 00:01:59.000
<v David>That's the right question, and the answer's already on the screen.

28
00:01:59.370 --> 00:02:10.680
<v David>When an out of network claim goes to federal arbitration in the United States, the middle award is 525 percent of the plan's own qualifying payment amount.

29
00:02:11.390 --> 00:02:15.280
<v David>That's the plan's own middle contracted rate for that service.

30
00:02:15.730 --> 00:02:17.040
<v David>Not a billed charge.

31
00:02:17.650 --> 00:02:18.980
<v David>Not the plan's offer.

32
00:02:19.430 --> 00:02:24.230
<v David>And every point above a hundred is money the plan didn't budget.

33
00:02:23.590 --> 00:02:26.280
<v Blair>Where's that number from?

34
00:02:25.360 --> 00:02:30.020
<v David>The federal public file of arbitration decisions.

35
00:02:30.970 --> 00:02:33.950
<v David>Four point three million dispute line items.

36
00:02:35.270 --> 00:02:39.990
<v David>There's no year printed on that page, so I don't give you one.

37
00:02:39.480 --> 00:02:44.820
<v Blair>Why does the bottom of that fraction matter so much to you?

38
00:02:43.940 --> 00:02:47.270
<v David>Because we had it wrong for a while, and we caught it ourselves.

39
00:02:48.090 --> 00:02:49.620
<v David>One emergency room visit.

40
00:02:50.140 --> 00:02:54.390
<v David>The plan's own rate was 203 dollars and 74 cents.

41
00:02:55.100 --> 00:02:58.460
<v David>The award was 608 dollars and 40 cents.

42
00:02:59.080 --> 00:03:01.040
<v David>That's 299 percent.

43
00:03:01.560 --> 00:03:06.820
<v David>Measure the same award against what the plan offered instead, and it's 210 percent.

44
00:03:07.310 --> 00:03:08.120
<v David>Same award.

45
00:03:08.480 --> 00:03:09.650
<v David>Two different numbers.

46
00:03:10.220 --> 00:03:11.950
<v David>So now we print which one it is.

47
00:03:12.020 --> 00:03:13.890
<v David>Don't go looking for that on the screen.

48
00:03:14.390 --> 00:03:18.860
<v David>That's the story of a bug we found in our own page.

49
00:03:18.750 --> 00:03:21.530
<v Blair>And 525 is national.

50
00:03:22.840 --> 00:03:25.070
<v Blair>My book is here.

51
00:03:24.170 --> 00:03:27.770
<v David>Then say California out loud, because the screen shortens it.

52
00:03:28.110 --> 00:03:36.320
<v David>Here the middle award is 279 percent, on forty-seven thousand three hundred ninety-seven dispute line items.

53
00:03:36.730 --> 00:03:42.140
<v David>The middle half lands between 242 and 330 percent.

54
00:03:41.760 --> 00:03:42.440
<v Blair>Hang on.

55
00:03:43.030 --> 00:03:48.730
<v Blair>Your dropdown says California, 81,409 line items.

56
00:03:48.980 --> 00:03:52.910
<v Blair>Your panel says 47,397.

57
00:03:53.240 --> 00:03:56.480
<v Blair>You just changed your own number in front of me.

58
00:03:56.070 --> 00:03:56.700
<v David>Good eye.

59
00:03:57.660 --> 00:04:02.060
<v David>The bigger number is every dispute line California produced.

60
00:04:03.010 --> 00:04:08.600
<v David>The smaller one is the rows that cleared our floor, and it's the one we headline.

61
00:04:09.310 --> 00:04:13.160
<v David>I'd rather show you the smaller number and tell you why.

62
00:04:13.550 --> 00:04:17.130
<v Blair>Most people would show you the big one.

63
00:04:16.090 --> 00:04:17.120
<v David>That's the whole point.

64
00:04:17.180 --> 00:04:26.130
<v David>Everyone in this category will either blur the number until you book a demo, or hand you a number and never tell you what it's made of.

65
00:04:27.080 --> 00:04:33.830
<v David>We print the number, the sample size, the date, and the rows we refused to print.

66
00:04:34.620 --> 00:04:35.480
<v Blair>Alright.

67
00:04:36.310 --> 00:04:38.260
<v Blair>My book's in Los Angeles.

68
00:04:38.580 --> 00:04:41.870
<v Blair>Do you have prospects for me there?

69
00:04:42.110 --> 00:04:42.870
<v David>Not today.

70
00:04:43.080 --> 00:04:47.750
<v David>That directory is Sacramento only, and the page says so on its own face.

71
00:04:48.270 --> 00:04:54.470
<v David>Sacramento, Roseville, Rocklin, Citrus Heights, Antelope.

72
00:04:54.930 --> 00:05:02.380
<v David>Switch it to another market and the page closes, rather than show you Sacramento under somebody else's name.

73
00:05:02.620 --> 00:05:06.910
<v David>Ask us for your market and we'll tell you honestly whether we can build one.

74
00:05:07.010 --> 00:05:09.330
<v Blair>That's a small map.

75
00:05:08.690 --> 00:05:09.290
<v David>It is.

76
00:05:10.000 --> 00:05:13.300
<v David>It's also three thousand and thirty named organizations.

77
00:05:14.250 --> 00:05:17.260
<v David>Two hundred seventeen carry a filed contract.

78
00:05:17.710 --> 00:05:19.760
<v David>Two hundred ten named carriers.

79
00:05:20.590 --> 00:05:23.770
<v David>A hundred ninety-eight name a broker of record on the filing.

80
00:05:24.480 --> 00:05:27.720
<v David>And one of those five cities is Citrus Heights.

81
00:05:28.420 --> 00:05:30.050
<v David>That's the room we're standing in.

82
00:05:30.350 --> 00:05:33.460
<v Blair>So what's missing from it?

83
00:05:32.170 --> 00:05:34.190
<v David>Government and church employers.

84
00:05:34.900 --> 00:05:35.630
<v David>All of them.

85
00:05:36.080 --> 00:05:41.030
<v David>Federal law exempts them from filing, so they're not in the record at all.

86
00:05:41.480 --> 00:05:47.740
<v David>In a state capital that's a big hole, and it's a hole in the source, not a hole in our search.

87
00:05:48.340 --> 00:05:56.880
<v David>And those employer filings run twelve to twenty-two months behind, so the broker named on one is who was on it then.

88
00:05:57.920 --> 00:06:00.540
<v Blair>Now the one everybody at this show is selling.

89
00:06:01.850 --> 00:06:02.840
<v Blair>Pharmacy.

90
00:06:04.150 --> 00:06:06.650
<v Blair>What's your number?

91
00:06:05.340 --> 00:06:06.420
<v David>We don't have one.

92
00:06:06.740 --> 00:06:12.830
<v David>We hold no pharmacy benefit manager contract, no rebate data, no point of sale pricing.

93
00:06:13.430 --> 00:06:15.840
<v David>We'll tell you nothing about your pharmacy benefit.

94
00:06:17.140 --> 00:06:24.520
<v David>And anyone selling you a pharmacy number off public files should be asked which file it came from.

95
00:06:24.860 --> 00:06:28.390
<v Blair>That's an odd thing to sound proud of.

96
00:06:27.790 --> 00:06:28.720
<v David>Here's why.

97
00:06:29.620 --> 00:06:32.430
<v David>There's another half of drug spend that is public.

98
00:06:32.630 --> 00:06:34.430
<v David>The drugs given in a clinic.

99
00:06:34.570 --> 00:06:40.860
<v David>Infusions and injections, billed under the medical benefit at a negotiated rate.

100
00:06:41.770 --> 00:06:46.830
<v David>They never touch a rebate structure at all, and almost nobody's looking at them.

101
00:06:46.850 --> 00:06:48.480
<v David>We hold those rates already.

102
00:06:48.520 --> 00:06:50.220
<v David>They're not in the lookup yet.

103
00:06:50.420 --> 00:06:52.610
<v Blair>Why not?

104
00:06:51.550 --> 00:06:56.730
<v David>Because a drug price with the wrong unit is worse than no drug price.

105
00:06:57.310 --> 00:07:00.550
<v David>One of these is billed in ten milligram units.

106
00:07:00.610 --> 00:07:03.520
<v David>A course is three to four hundred milligrams.

107
00:07:04.030 --> 00:07:08.140
<v David>Read one as the other and you're off by thirty-five times.

108
00:07:08.640 --> 00:07:12.310
<v David>So it ships when the unit is right, and not before.

109
00:07:12.690 --> 00:07:15.940
<v Blair>And if somebody pushes anyway?

110
00:07:14.750 --> 00:07:16.360
<v David>The product answers for me.

111
00:07:17.670 --> 00:07:24.550
<v David>Ask it what a drug code costs and it says there is nothing honest to price.

112
00:07:24.470 --> 00:07:26.410
<v Blair>Now do it with no screen.

113
00:07:27.230 --> 00:07:31.490
<v Blair>Someone walks up cold, coffee in hand.

114
00:07:32.810 --> 00:07:33.830
<v Blair>I'll be her.

115
00:07:35.140 --> 00:07:35.800
<v Blair>Hi.

116
00:07:36.920 --> 00:07:39.380
<v Blair>What do you do?

117
00:07:39.580 --> 00:07:42.390
<v David>Most health plans have to file their contracted rates.

118
00:07:42.430 --> 00:07:43.580
<v David>The files are public.

119
00:07:43.610 --> 00:07:44.310
<v David>And huge.

120
00:07:44.920 --> 00:07:47.190
<v David>Almost nobody on the buying side opens one.

121
00:07:48.020 --> 00:07:48.810
<v David>We read them.

122
00:07:49.920 --> 00:07:52.390
<v David>That's the whole company.

123
00:07:52.730 --> 00:07:53.400
<v Blair>Huh.

124
00:07:54.360 --> 00:07:56.820
<v Blair>How does that help us?

125
00:07:55.790 --> 00:07:57.200
<v David>Same knee operation.

126
00:07:57.390 --> 00:07:58.460
<v David>Same patient.

127
00:07:59.010 --> 00:07:59.990
<v David>Sacramento.

128
00:08:00.470 --> 00:08:02.540
<v David>In an office it's five forty-one.

129
00:08:02.900 --> 00:08:05.790
<v David>In a surgery center, two thousand one eighty-six.

130
00:08:06.270 --> 00:08:09.150
<v David>In a hospital, three thousand eight hundred eighty-four.

131
00:08:09.900 --> 00:08:12.380
<v David>Moving one case is sixteen ninety-eight.

132
00:08:12.920 --> 00:08:14.800
<v David>That's off the federal schedules.

133
00:08:15.630 --> 00:08:17.140
<v David>And here's the honest part.

134
00:08:18.090 --> 00:08:20.370
<v David>The doctor's half is priced for this area.

135
00:08:20.750 --> 00:08:23.420
<v David>The building half is the national amount.

136
00:08:24.240 --> 00:08:27.130
<v David>I'd rather say that than let you find it.

137
00:08:26.760 --> 00:08:27.600
<v Blair>Hang on.

138
00:08:28.710 --> 00:08:30.040
<v Blair>That's a real number.

139
00:08:30.750 --> 00:08:33.030
<v Blair>What do you charge for it?

140
00:08:32.240 --> 00:08:34.750
<v David>A seat's a hundred forty-nine a month.

141
00:08:35.200 --> 00:08:37.560
<v David>Or fourteen ninety for the year.

142
00:08:38.260 --> 00:08:42.720
<v David>A whole firm is forty-nine hundred a year, flat, not per person.

143
00:08:43.420 --> 00:08:44.720
<v David>One flat fee.

144
00:08:45.680 --> 00:08:47.380
<v David>Never a share of anything.

145
00:08:47.750 --> 00:08:52.920
<v David>A fee that grows with someone's number gives us a reason to prefer one answer.

146
00:08:53.370 --> 00:08:55.030
<v David>We'd rather not have one.

147
00:08:55.360 --> 00:08:59.180
<v David>And you can read all of that on the site without booking a call.

148
00:08:59.670 --> 00:09:00.330
<v Blair>Right.

149
00:09:01.640 --> 00:09:04.540
<v Blair>So why is it valuable?

150
00:09:04.710 --> 00:09:12.260
<v David>We print the number, the sample size, the date, and the rows we refused to print.

151
00:09:13.090 --> 00:09:18.550
<v David>And where there isn't an honest number, you get a sentence instead of a figure.

152
00:09:17.840 --> 00:09:21.320
<v Blair>How does that land in a meeting?

153
00:09:20.130 --> 00:09:23.980
<v David>Your client's being told their increase is what the market's doing.

154
00:09:24.580 --> 00:09:25.800
<v David>Now they can check that.

155
00:09:26.250 --> 00:09:32.770
<v David>The conversation stops being about your commission and starts being about their spend.

156
00:09:33.110 --> 00:09:33.690
<v Blair>Right.

157
00:09:34.800 --> 00:09:36.070
<v Blair>I think I'm interested.

158
00:09:36.890 --> 00:09:39.150
<v Blair>Can you talk to my team?

159
00:09:38.460 --> 00:09:39.230
<v David>Happy to.

160
00:09:39.780 --> 00:09:41.040
<v David>Do two things first.

161
00:09:41.170 --> 00:09:43.620
<v David>Open the public page on your phone at that table.

162
00:09:44.260 --> 00:09:45.100
<v David>No login.

163
00:09:45.390 --> 00:09:47.500
<v David>Check us against a rate you already know.

164
00:09:48.250 --> 00:09:49.860
<v David>Then open the demo door.

165
00:09:50.500 --> 00:09:52.050
<v David>Press log in with demo.

166
00:09:52.530 --> 00:09:54.650
<v David>No email, nothing to cancel.

167
00:09:55.300 --> 00:10:00.140
<v David>After that we book a call and I'll bring a market your team actually works in.

168
00:10:00.400 --> 00:10:03.410
<v Blair>Anything I have to hand over?

169
00:10:03.640 --> 00:10:04.220
<v David>Nothing.

170
00:10:04.430 --> 00:10:05.990
<v David>No patient data, ever.

171
00:10:06.940 --> 00:10:11.210
<v David>It all comes off files the health plans publish themselves.

172
00:10:10.970 --> 00:10:14.910
<v Blair>Four different people are going to stop at that booth.

173
00:10:16.020 --> 00:10:17.470
<v Blair>Start with the broker.

174
00:10:18.790 --> 00:10:21.430
<v Blair>Say she's got a client meeting Thursday.

175
00:10:22.380 --> 00:10:25.690
<v Blair>What does she walk in with?

176
00:10:24.430 --> 00:10:27.060
<v David>A public page she can open on her phone at the table.

177
00:10:27.810 --> 00:10:29.610
<v David>A brain scan in Sacramento.

178
00:10:30.470 --> 00:10:32.690
<v David>The middle price is five ninety-two.

179
00:10:33.560 --> 00:10:36.380
<v David>Half that market sits above it, half below.

180
00:10:37.120 --> 00:10:42.940
<v David>Twenty-two thousand two hundred thirty-two filings behind it, as of July twentieth.

181
00:10:44.050 --> 00:10:49.510
<v David>And that gap is between contracts, not between quality.

182
00:10:48.440 --> 00:10:51.320
<v Blair>And inside the product?

183
00:10:50.400 --> 00:10:53.460
<v David>A sheet with twelve named plans for this market.

184
00:10:54.240 --> 00:10:56.800
<v David>Underneath, it counts what it held back.

185
00:10:57.850 --> 00:10:59.250
<v David>Six cells held back.

186
00:11:00.150 --> 00:11:04.990
<v David>Twenty-three plans left out, because their book belongs to another state.

187
00:11:05.560 --> 00:11:07.440
<v David>That sheet is hers to read.

188
00:11:08.020 --> 00:11:12.470
<v David>What she sends a client is the price sheet or the question set.

189
00:11:13.180 --> 00:11:18.260
<v David>Those open with no account, and there's nothing on them to click.

190
00:11:17.620 --> 00:11:21.150
<v Blair>Does one carry her firm's name?

191
00:11:20.120 --> 00:11:23.800
<v David>On her own seat it will carry her firm's name across the top.

192
00:11:24.260 --> 00:11:26.010
<v David>The demo one carries ours.

193
00:11:26.720 --> 00:11:29.640
<v David>Say that out loud, because somebody's going to look.

194
00:11:29.980 --> 00:11:32.580
<v Blair>Which plans are on the sheet?

195
00:11:31.890 --> 00:11:33.340
<v David>Twelve that file here.

196
00:11:34.650 --> 00:11:39.680
<v David>And before you ask, we don't hold Blue Shield at volume yet.

197
00:11:40.510 --> 00:11:41.700
<v David>We're acquiring it.

198
00:11:42.650 --> 00:11:48.980
<v David>I'm not going to give you a California number built on one carrier and call it the market.

199
00:11:48.010 --> 00:11:53.860
<v Blair>In this room somebody's going to say Kaiser.

200
00:11:54.280 --> 00:11:55.750
<v David>Say it before they do.

201
00:11:56.550 --> 00:12:03.580
<v David>Kaiser doesn't publish negotiated rates in the form this product reads, because it doesn't negotiate them.

202
00:12:04.080 --> 00:12:06.610
<v David>Care happens inside its own system.

203
00:12:07.410 --> 00:12:10.250
<v David>So nothing built on these files can price Kaiser.

204
00:12:11.340 --> 00:12:12.860
<v David>Ours included.

205
00:12:12.690 --> 00:12:17.290
<v Blair>And the thing she'll ask you for that you don't have yet?

206
00:12:16.690 --> 00:12:20.880
<v David>Her own book on the screen, ranked by renewal date.

207
00:12:22.200 --> 00:12:24.510
<v David>That's the one brokers ask for most.

208
00:12:25.820 --> 00:12:27.120
<v David>We haven't built it.

209
00:12:27.640 --> 00:12:29.970
<v David>That one's coming.

210
00:12:30.330 --> 00:12:31.230
<v Blair>Next.

211
00:12:32.540 --> 00:12:35.630
<v Blair>The general agency.

212
00:12:34.480 --> 00:12:36.840
<v David>This is the one I get most excited about.

213
00:12:37.660 --> 00:12:50.730
<v David>Every employer with a hundred people on a plan files a federal form called the fifty five hundred, every year, and it names their carrier, their broker of record, and what that broker was paid.

214
00:12:51.340 --> 00:12:54.300
<v David>The government publishes all of it and nobody reads it.

215
00:12:54.510 --> 00:12:59.230
<v David>We read every one, every state, every year on record.

216
00:12:59.720 --> 00:13:04.180
<v Blair>So what does a general agency actually get?

217
00:13:03.080 --> 00:13:06.260
<v David>Their whole territory, described by itself.

218
00:13:07.350 --> 00:13:15.050
<v David>Point a producer at any market and the register hands them every employer in it sorted by renewal month.

219
00:13:16.330 --> 00:13:17.750
<v David>The incumbent carrier.

220
00:13:18.680 --> 00:13:23.900
<v David>The incumbent broker and what they were paid last year, to the dollar.

221
00:13:24.110 --> 00:13:29.880
<v David>The plan administrator's name, the person inside that company who owns the health plan decision.

222
00:13:30.580 --> 00:13:36.540
<v David>The accounting firm that prepared the filing, so you know who advises them on money before you call.

223
00:13:37.250 --> 00:13:39.800
<v David>And the phone number off the filing.

224
00:13:40.410 --> 00:13:46.870
<v David>Twenty thousand two hundred of the twenty thousand four hundred California filings carry one.

225
00:13:46.800 --> 00:13:49.450
<v Blair>How did you pull that off?

226
00:13:48.190 --> 00:13:49.910
<v David>Honestly, the files were sitting there.

227
00:13:50.810 --> 00:14:02.760
<v David>What did not exist was the machine to read a hundred fifty thousand of them, join every employer to the rate record, and put a renewal clock on every row.

228
00:14:03.200 --> 00:14:04.190
<v David>We built that.

229
00:14:05.100 --> 00:14:09.430
<v David>In California alone, eight thousand four hundred employers.

230
00:14:10.550 --> 00:14:14.240
<v David>Three hundred forty eight in Sacramento.

231
00:14:13.350 --> 00:14:16.190
<v Blair>And the price for the firm?

232
00:14:15.150 --> 00:14:17.910
<v David>Forty-nine hundred a year, flat.

233
00:14:18.120 --> 00:14:19.050
<v David>Not per seat.

234
00:14:19.570 --> 00:14:21.570
<v David>Up to ten named producers.

235
00:14:22.280 --> 00:14:26.520
<v David>If the firm's a big one, we size it on the call.

236
00:14:25.740 --> 00:14:26.530
<v Blair>Third.

237
00:14:27.350 --> 00:14:29.960
<v Blair>The self-funded employer.

238
00:14:29.540 --> 00:14:32.110
<v David>He's who those knee numbers are actually about.

239
00:14:32.430 --> 00:14:36.620
<v David>Same operation, three buildings, sixteen ninety-eight between two of them.

240
00:14:37.080 --> 00:14:39.000
<v David>That's his money, not the broker's.

241
00:14:39.460 --> 00:14:43.670
<v David>And that 279 percent isn't the broker's problem either.

242
00:14:44.120 --> 00:14:47.750
<v David>A self-funded plan pays that award out of its own money.

243
00:14:48.130 --> 00:14:49.400
<v David>He never buys a seat.

244
00:14:49.440 --> 00:14:51.140
<v David>It reaches him through his broker.

245
00:14:51.440 --> 00:14:56.650
<v Blair>And if most of a broker's book is fully insured?

246
00:14:55.200 --> 00:14:56.730
<v David>Part of this still works.

247
00:14:57.230 --> 00:15:03.070
<v David>The market page and the building to building numbers don't care how a group is funded.

248
00:15:03.510 --> 00:15:06.500
<v David>They come off public files and federal schedules.

249
00:15:07.190 --> 00:15:13.640
<v David>The employer directory is built from federal plan filings, so that half is narrower.

250
00:15:14.030 --> 00:15:17.210
<v David>Start with the market page on a group you've already got.

251
00:15:17.420 --> 00:15:22.070
<v Blair>And someone from a health plan stops by.

252
00:15:21.030 --> 00:15:22.870
<v David>There's nothing to sell them today.

253
00:15:23.480 --> 00:15:25.570
<v David>No price, no checkout.

254
00:15:26.520 --> 00:15:30.610
<v David>They can read their own filed rates next to the market, same as anyone.

255
00:15:31.920 --> 00:15:37.980
<v David>What we do with that group is still open, and it's better to say so.

256
00:15:36.900 --> 00:15:38.170
<v Blair>Here's the hard one.

257
00:15:39.480 --> 00:15:45.670
<v Blair>What stops someone deciding you're just another data outfit?

258
00:15:44.530 --> 00:15:45.770
<v David>The empty screens.

259
00:15:46.300 --> 00:15:50.210
<v David>Ask for Santa Rosa and it refuses in half a second.

260
00:15:50.920 --> 00:15:59.100
<v David>It says our floor is a hundred distinct providers behind every figure, and we don't lower it to fill a page.

261
00:15:59.930 --> 00:16:05.060
<v David>Ask for Chico and every filing we hold there belongs to another state's book.

262
00:16:05.450 --> 00:16:10.090
<v Blair>You show people the questions you won't answer.

263
00:16:08.940 --> 00:16:13.900
<v David>Two ordinary office visits in Sacramento have seventeen filings and twenty.

264
00:16:14.190 --> 00:16:15.180
<v David>We need a hundred.

265
00:16:15.630 --> 00:16:22.030
<v David>So we refuse the Sacramento cell, show California instead, and label that row statewide.

266
00:16:22.410 --> 00:16:25.990
<v David>An empty result on our site is the product working correctly.

267
00:16:26.360 --> 00:16:29.100
<v Blair>Give me one more.

268
00:16:28.030 --> 00:16:30.640
<v David>Our answer cards count their own gaps.

269
00:16:31.090 --> 00:16:36.710
<v David>One says a further point is held off the card, because nobody has read the source text yet.

270
00:16:37.420 --> 00:16:41.640
<v David>It tells you which of its own points it hasn't finished checking.

271
00:16:41.360 --> 00:16:42.980
<v Blair>Zero customers.

272
00:16:43.810 --> 00:16:46.650
<v Blair>That's what someone's going to hear.

273
00:16:46.450 --> 00:16:47.520
<v David>That's what it is.

274
00:16:48.040 --> 00:16:52.550
<v David>So the price is public, and the numbers are in front of you before you pay us anything.

275
00:16:52.930 --> 00:16:54.160
<v David>Check the number first.

276
00:16:54.680 --> 00:16:57.410
<v David>The order matters more than the count.

277
00:16:57.790 --> 00:16:58.720
<v Blair>Last one.

278
00:17:00.040 --> 00:17:03.460
<v Blair>What do you want them to leave with?

279
00:17:03.680 --> 00:17:04.380
<v David>One thing.

280
00:17:05.690 --> 00:17:07.450
<v David>Nobody has bought a seat yet.

281
00:17:08.400 --> 00:17:11.760
<v David>You'd be the first, and I'd sit with you while you did it.

282
00:17:12.580 --> 00:17:14.440
<v David>So don't take our word for any of it.

283
00:17:15.750 --> 00:17:17.680
<v David>Check us on a rate you already know.

284
00:17:18.280 --> 00:17:20.379
<v David>If we're wrong, you'll find it in a minute.
